import json
P='/workspace/sites/olympiad-atlas/research/oceania/'
items=[]; ledger=[]
def url(y):
 return {2016:'https://amt.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/2016-AMO-Paper-and-Solutions.pdf',2017:'https://amt.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/2017-AMO-Paper-and-Solutions.pdf',2018:'https://amt.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/02/AMO-2018-Problems-and-Solutions.pdf',2019:'https://amt.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/AMO-2019-paper-and-solutions.pdf',2020:'https://amt.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/AMO-2020-paper-and-solutions.pdf'}.get(y,f'https://enrichedmaths.maths.uwa.edu.au/Olympiad/AMO/AMO{y}wsoln.pdf')
def add(y,n,d,topic,tags,statement,solution,reason):
 id=f'aus_amo_{y}_{n}'
 items.append(dict(id=id,country='Australia',competition='Australian Mathematical Olympiad',year=y,number=str(n),statement=statement,solution=solution or 'The linked paper contains the official worked solution. Open “Source solution” below to read it.',sourceUrl=url(y),solutionUrl=url(y),topic=topic,tags=tags,difficulty=d,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',solutionKind='original-exposition' if solution else 'source-link',notes='Australian Mathematics Trust problem. Mathematical statement paraphrased; displayed exposition independently written. Difficulty assessed from this problem and its solution.'))
 ledger.append(dict(id=id,country='Australia',competition='Australian Mathematical Olympiad',year=y,number=str(n),difficulty=d,status='reviewed',included=True,reason=reason,sourceUrl=url(y)))
def skip(y,n,d,reason): ledger.append(dict(id=f'aus_amo_{y}_{n}',country='Australia',competition='Australian Mathematical Olympiad',year=y,number=str(n),difficulty=d,status='reviewed' if d else 'pending-solution-review',included=False,reason=reason,sourceUrl=url(y)))
add(2023,1,4,'Combinatorics',['construction','parity','arithmetic progressions'],r'For which integers $n>2$ can the edges of an $n$-gon be labelled $1,\ldots,n$, each once, so that the sums of the two labels meeting at each vertex form an arithmetic progression after rearrangement?',r'''Exactly the odd integers work. The sum of the vertex sums is $n(n+1)$. If the progression begins at $a$ and has difference $d\ge0$, then $2a+(n-1)d=2n+2$. Since $a\ge3$, a positive difference must be $d=1$. Difference zero is impossible: equal adjacent sums would force the edge labels to repeat every two edges. Thus $a=(n+3)/2$, so $n$ is odd.

For $n=2k-1$, place the edge labels cyclically in the order
$$k,\ 2k-1,\ k-1,\ 2k-2,\ k-2,\ldots,\ k+1,\ 1.$$
Their neighbouring sums are precisely $k+1,k+2,\ldots,3k-1$.''','The sum constraint gives necessity, but an explicit cyclic construction is also needed.')
skip(2023,2,2,'Toggling membership of 1 and 2 distributes subset sums equally among the four residues.')
add(2023,3,6.5,'Algebra',['polynomials','construction','coefficient signs'],r'Given any nonzero real polynomial $f$, prove that some real polynomial $g$ makes $fg$ have exactly $2023$ more positive coefficients than negative coefficients. Zero coefficients do not count.',r'''Write the lowest and highest nonzero terms of $f$ as $c_a x^a,c_bx^b$. If $a=b$, the claim follows immediately by multiplying by a suitably signed sum of $2023$ monomials. Otherwise choose $\lambda>0$ with $c_a-\lambda c_b\ne0$, and put $h=x^{b-a}-\lambda$.

In $hf$, the low copy of the coefficients of $f$ has its signs reversed, while the high copy retains them. Apart from their shared endpoint, these sign counts cancel. Consequently, the number of positive coefficients minus negative coefficients in $hf$ is
$$\operatorname{sgn}(c_a-\lambda c_b)-\operatorname{sgn}(c_a)+\operatorname{sgn}(c_b),$$
which is either $1$ or $-1$. Choose a sign $\varepsilon$ making it $1$. If $D>\deg(hf)$, then
$$g=\varepsilon h(1+x^D+x^{2D}+\cdots+x^{2022D})$$
creates $2023$ disjoint copies of the coefficient list, giving the result.''','A carefully shifted difference cancels almost every coefficient sign; separated copies then amplify the imbalance.')
add(2023,4,6,'Geometry',['cyclic quadrilaterals','homothety','angle chasing'],r'In acute triangle $ABC$, let $D$ be the foot of the altitude from $A$. Choose $P\in AB$ and $Q\in AC$ with $PQ\parallel BC$, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $PQ$. Let $X$ be the intersection of segment $DM$ with circle $APQ$ lying inside $ABC$. Prove $\angle AXB=\angle AXC$.',None,'Extending PX and QX to the base uncovers a homothety, equal distances from D, and two further cyclic quadrilaterals.')
add(2023,5,4,'Number Theory',['sums','squares','contradiction'],r'A finite interval of positive integers has this property: whenever two subsets $C,U$ satisfy $|C|=|U|+1$, their sums satisfy $\sum C>\sum U$. The subsets may overlap. Prove that the interval contains at most one perfect square.',r'''If it contained two squares, it would contain $x^2$ and $(x+1)^2$ for some positive integer $x$, together with all intervening integers. Take
$$C=\{x^2,x^2+1,\ldots,x^2+x\},\qquad U=\{x^2+x+1,\ldots,x^2+2x\}.$$
Their sizes differ by one, but
$$\sum C=(x+1)x^2+\frac{x(x+1)}2
= x(x^2+x)+\frac{x(x+1)}2=\sum U,$$
a contradiction.''','The central insight is to build two equal-sum blocks between consecutive squares with sizes differing by one.')
add(2023,6,4,'Geometry',['coordinates','circumcentres','invariants'],r'Fix a parallelogram $ABCD$. A point $E$ moves along the interior of $AB$. If $K,L$ are the circumcentres of $ADE,BCE$, respectively, prove that the length $KL$ is constant.',r'''Choose $A=(0,0),B=(s,0),D=(u,v),C=(s+u,v)$, with $v\ne0$, and put $E=(t,0)$. The perpendicular bisectors of $AE$ and $BE$ give the horizontal coordinates of the two centres. Equating their squared distances from the other vertices gives
$$K=\left(\frac t2,\frac{u^2+v^2-ut}{2v}\right),\quad L=\left(\frac{s+t}2,\frac{u^2+v^2+us-ut}{2v}\right).$$
Therefore $L-K=(s/2,us/(2v))$, independent of $t$.''','The useful invariant becomes visible after computing two circumcentres in the same coordinate system.')
add(2023,7,5.5,'Number Theory',['divisors','binary representation','construction'],r'For every positive integer $n$, prove that at least $2023$ positive integers $k$ have their divisors partitionable into two sets $A,B$ with $\sum_{d\in A}d-\sum_{d\in B}d=n$.',r'''In fact there are infinitely many such $k$. Signed sums of $1,2,\ldots,2^r$ attain every odd integer between $-(2^{r+1}-1)$ and $2^{r+1}-1$: choose the positive terms to have binary subset sum $(n+2^{r+1}-1)/2$. Thus odd $n$ works with every sufficiently large $k=2^r$.

For even $n$, use $k=3\cdot2^r$. Its divisors form two disjoint lists, $2^i$ and $3\cdot2^i$ for $0\le i\le r$. Give the first list signs summing to the odd number $n-3$, possible for all sufficiently large $r$. On the second list use the signs
$$3\cdot2^r-3(1+2+\cdots+2^{r-1})=3.$$
Together the signed divisor sum is $n$.''','Two different divisor constructions are needed for odd and even targets; binary signed sums provide the common mechanism.')
add(2023,8,6.5,'Combinatorics',['games','adversarial strategy','optimization'],r'The cards $1,2,\ldots,2023$ are hidden in an unknown order. Before each card is revealed, choose a positive integer $t$. Score $t$ if that card is at least $t$, and zero otherwise. What is the largest total score that can be guaranteed, allowing choices to depend on previous cards?',r'''The answer is $1012^2$. Always choosing $1012$ earns this amount, since exactly $1012$ cards meet the threshold.

For the upper bound, an adversary reveals the smallest remaining card whenever it is below the chosen threshold. Otherwise it reveals the largest remaining card, and the turn scores at most the smallest remaining value. The cards revealed on successful turns therefore form a descending list $2023,2022,\ldots,b$ for some $b$. On every such turn the score is at most $b$, which has not yet been removed. Hence the total is at most
$$b(2024-b)\le1012^2.$$
For any fixed adaptive strategy these choices produce one valid ordering of the deck, so the bound applies to every strategy.''','A matching adversarial upper bound is substantially harder than the constant-threshold lower bound.')
skip(2024,1,2.5,'Factorial divisibility and a short comparison reduce the possibilities directly.')
add(2024,2,4.5,'Geometry',['incentres','cyclic quadrilaterals','angle bisectors'],r'Let $ABCD$ be cyclic. On ray $CB$ beyond $B$ choose $P$ with $CP=CA$, and on ray $CD$ beyond $D$ choose $Q$ with $CQ=CA$. Prove that the incentre of triangle $ABD$ lies on $PQ$.',None,'The equal-radius construction and two half-angle cyclicity relations locate the incentre on the desired line.')
add(2024,3,4.5,'Algebra',['inequalities','rearrangements','telescoping'],r'Let $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ be positive real numbers. The score of a permutation $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ is $\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}b_i^2/b_{i+1}$. Prove that two permutations have scores differing by at least $3|a_1-a_n|$.',r'''Sort the numbers as $x_1\ge\cdots\ge x_n$, and compare this order with its reverse. Their score difference is
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\left(\frac{x_i^2}{x_{i+1}}-\frac{x_{i+1}^2}{x_i}\right)
=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(x_i-x_{i+1})\frac{x_i^2+x_ix_{i+1}+x_{i+1}^2}{x_ix_{i+1}}.$$
Since the last fraction is at least $3$, this is at least $3(x_1-x_n)\ge3|a_1-a_n|$.''','Choosing opposite sorted orders converts a global permutation claim into a telescoping local inequality.')
add(2024,4,6.5,'Combinatorics',['grid colouring','linear algebra','recurrences'],r'How many black-and-white colourings of a $2024\times2024$ grid have an even number of black side-neighbours at every cell? Only cells inside the grid are counted as neighbours.',r'''More generally an $n\times n$ grid has $2^n$ such colourings. Work over $\mathbf F_2$, encode a black cell by $1$, and write row $i$ as a column vector $r_i$. Let $A$ be the adjacency matrix of the path with $n$ vertices, and set the outside rows $r_0=r_{n+1}=0$. The condition is
$$r_{i+1}=Ar_i+r_{i-1}.$$
Thus the first row determines every other row. It remains to check that every first row produces the required final zero row.

Let $D_j(x)$ be the characteristic polynomial of the path adjacency matrix of size $j$. Expansion of the tridiagonal determinant gives $D_0=1,D_1=x,D_j=xD_{j-1}+D_{j-2}$ over $\mathbf F_2$. The row recurrence gives $r_{n+1}=D_n(A)r_1=0$ by the Cayley–Hamilton theorem. All $2^n$ first rows therefore work; the answer is $2^{2024}$.''','The first-row recurrence is easy to spot; proving the opposite boundary always closes needs a nontrivial symmetry or polynomial-matrix argument.')
add(2024,5,4,'Number Theory',['prime factors','recurrences','bounding'],r'Start with $a_1=2^{2024}+1$. For $n\ge1$, let $a_{n+1}$ be the largest prime factor of $a_n^2-1$. Find $a_{2024}+a_{2025}$.',r'''The answer is $5$. We have
$$a_1^2-1=2^{2025}(2^{2023}+1),$$
so $a_2\le2^{2023}+1$. Every subsequent term is prime. For an odd prime $p\ge5$, every prime divisor of $(p-1)(p+1)$ is at most $(p+1)/2$, because both factors are even. The exceptional primes satisfy $2\mapsto3\mapsto2$.

Until the sequence enters this two-cycle, induction therefore gives $a_n\le2^{2025-n}+1$. In particular, by index $2024$ it has reached a prime at most $3$. Its next two terms are consequently $2$ and $3$ in some order.''','Factorization gives a repeated halving bound, with the terminal two-cycle handled separately.')
add(2024,6,5,'Combinatorics',['pigeonhole principle','greedy construction','residue classes'],r'A school has $1000$ pupils in each of years $1,\ldots,12$, and lockers numbered $1,\ldots,12000$. Can lockers be assigned bijectively so that any two pupils in year $k$ receive numbers whose difference is divisible by $k$?',r'''Yes. Assign the years in descending order. When year $k$ is reached, exactly $1000k$ lockers remain. Their numbers lie in $k$ residue classes modulo $k$, so at least one class contains $1000$ of them. Assign any $1000$ lockers from this class to year $k$ and continue. This completes all twelve years and uses every locker.''','The concise solution depends on recognizing the correct descending greedy order and preserving the exact count of remaining lockers.')
add(2024,7,5.5,'Geometry',['rotations','orthocentres','concurrency'],r'In square $ABCD$, take $P\in AB$ and a point $Q$ outside the square with $\angle ABQ=\angle ADP$ and $\angle AQB=90^\circ$. Choose $R\in BC$ so that $\angle BAR=\angle ADQ$. Prove that $AR,CQ,DP$ are concurrent.',None,'A quarter-turn creates a triangle in which the three proposed concurrent lines become altitudes.')
skip(2024,8,7.5,'The irrationality of the limiting reversed idempotent expansion needs a longer valuation and digit-periodicity argument.')
add(2022,1,4,'Geometry',['integer lengths','parity','right angles'],r'Consider convex pentagons whose side lengths are integers and whose perimeter is odd. Prove that such a pentagon can have two right angles, but cannot have more than two.',None,'A parity obstruction emerges after completing the three-right-angle configuration to an integer right triangle; attainability must also be constructed.')
add(2022,2,4.5,'Geometry',['vectors','areas','parallelograms'],r'A point $P$ lies inside convex hexagon $ABCDEF$. Suppose $PABC,PCDE,PEFA$ are parallelograms of equal area. Prove that $PBCD,PDEF,PFAB$ have equal area.',r'''Use $P$ as origin and let the position vectors of $A,C,E$ be $a,c,e$. The parallelograms give $B=a+c,D=c+e,F=e+a$. Their equal positively oriented areas give
$$a\times c=c\times e=e\times a.$$
It follows that $a+c+e$ has cross product zero with both $a$ and $c$. Since these vectors are independent, $a+c+e=0$. Thus $B=-e,D=-a,F=-c$. The remaining three quadrilaterals are parallelograms, and their areas are the same common cross product.''','Equal area relations must be translated into a vector dependence before the second set of areas becomes transparent.')
add(2022,3,4.5,'Algebra',['polynomials','limits','functional identities'],r'Find all real polynomials $P$ positive on the positive real numbers such that $1/P(x)+1/P(y)+1/P(z)=1$ whenever $x,y,z>0$ and $xyz=1$.',r'''The only solution is $P\equiv3$. Suppose instead that $P$ is nonconstant. Its positivity implies $P(t)\to+\infty$ as $t\to+\infty$. Taking $(x,y,z)=(t,t,t^{-2})$ shows that $1/P(t^{-2})\to1$, and hence continuity gives $P(0)=1$.

Now take $(x,y,z)=(t,1,t^{-1})$ and let $t\to\infty$. The identity becomes $0+1/P(1)+1=1$, contrary to $P(1)>0$. A constant polynomial is forced to equal $3$, which works.''','Two different degenerations of the multiplicative constraint produce incompatible limiting values for a nonconstant polynomial.')
add(2022,4,5.5,'Combinatorics',['tournaments','bijections','transitivity'],r'Colour every pair $(i,j)$ with $1\le i<j\le2022$ red or blue. Require that if $(i,j)$ and $(j,k)$ have the same colour, then $(i,k)$ has that colour too. How many colourings satisfy this?',r'''The answer is $2022!$. On vertices $1,\ldots,2022$, orient the edge from $i$ to $j$ for $i<j$ when the pair is red, and reverse it when blue. For any $i<j<k$, a directed triangle is exactly a violation of the stated colour condition.

A tournament without directed triangles has no directed cycle: a shortest cycle of length at least four has a chord, and one orientation creates a shorter cycle. An acyclic tournament therefore orders all vertices uniquely, with every edge directed forward. Conversely every ordering gives such a tournament. The colourings are consequently in bijection with the permutations.''','The key step is identifying the colouring rule with transitivity of a tournament, followed by a bijective count.')
skip(2022,5,2,'A weighted-sum comparison across the sign change immediately gives positivity.')
add(2022,6,4.5,'Combinatorics',['graphs','complement graphs','diameter'],r'Every pair of twenty towns is joined by a road coloured red or blue. Two towns have shortest red-route distance exactly four. Prove that any two towns can be joined by at most two blue roads.',r'''Suppose some towns $u,v$ cannot be joined by at most two blue roads. Their own edge must be red. Every other town has a red edge to at least one of $u,v$, since otherwise it would provide a two-edge blue route between them.

Any two towns can therefore be joined in red using their red connections to $\{u,v\}$ and, if necessary, the red edge $uv$. This takes at most three edges, contradicting the specified red distance four.''','A failed two-edge route in the complementary graph forces a dominating red edge and hence a diameter bound.')
