add(2021,6,4.5,'Combinatorics',['counterexamples','graphs','partitions'],r'Sixty pupils each name three different other pupils as friends. Must it be possible to split them into three classes of twenty so that every pupil shares a class with at least one of their named friends?',r'''No. Choose four special pupils, each naming the other three. Every remaining pupil names three of these four special pupils.

Any class containing an ordinary pupil must contain a special pupil. Moreover, a class containing any special pupil must contain at least two special pupils, since that pupil names only other special pupils. All three classes contain ordinary pupils, because each has twenty members and there are only four special pupils. Thus at least six special pupils would be required, a contradiction.''','A small mutually dependent core obstructs every balanced partition; finding the core is the main creative step.')
add(2021,7,5.5,'Number Theory',['recurrences','prime divisors','periodicity'],r'Let $a_1=1$ and $a_{n+1}=a_n^2+1$. Prove that a prime with more than $2021$ decimal digits divides some term of the sequence.',r'''It suffices to prove that infinitely many different primes occur. Introduce $a_0=0$, so the recurrence also holds at index zero. If a prime $p$ divides $a_k$, then modulo $p$ the orbit has returned to its starting value, and hence $a_{jk}\equiv0\pmod p$ for every positive integer $j$.

Suppose only finitely many primes $p_1,\ldots,p_s$ divide the positive terms. Choose $k_i$ with $p_i\mid a_{k_i}$, and let $L$ be a common multiple of the $k_i$. Then every $p_i$ divides $a_L$, so $a_{L+1}=a_L^2+1$ is divisible by none of them. Since $a_{L+1}>1$, this is impossible. Infinitely many prime divisors occur, and their numbers of digits are unbounded.''','A Euclidean contradiction is obtained by synchronizing return times of the recurrence modulo all hypothetical prime divisors.')
