add(2019,3,4.5,'Geometry',['circumcentres','cyclic quadrilaterals','isosceles trapezia'],r'Five points $A,B,C,D,E$ lie in this order on a circle, with $AB=CD$ and $BC=DE$. Put $P=AD\cap BE$. Prove that the circumcentre of $AEP$ lies on the original circle.',None,'The chord equalities create two isosceles trapezia, and an angle chase then identifies the new circumcentre on the old circle.')
add(2019,4,4.5,'Combinatorics',['convexity','pigeonhole principle','geometry'],r'Let $Q$ lie inside convex $1000$-gon $P_1\cdots P_{1000}$. The ray $P_iQ$ next meets its boundary at $X_i$, and none of these points is a vertex. Prove that some side contains no $X_i$.',r'''Cut the polygon along $P_1P_{501}$. The point $Q$ is not on this diagonal, and after reversing the labels if necessary it lies on the side containing $P_{1000}$.

For every $1\le i\le501$, the ray from $P_i$ through $Q$ exits the polygon on that same side of the diagonal. Thus the $501$ points $X_1,\ldots,X_{501}$ occupy only the $500$ sides from $P_{501}$ around through $P_{1000}$ to $P_1$. Two of them share a side. Since there are only $1000$ points in total and $1000$ sides, another side contains none.''','A diagonal through opposite vertices creates the necessary strict pigeonhole imbalance; local angle chasing is unnecessary.')
add(2019,5,4,'Number Theory',['modular arithmetic','triangular grids','colouring'],r'Place integers at the points of a triangular array with ten rows. Suppose the three entries at the vertices of every unit equilateral triangle sum to a multiple of $3$. What numbers of entries divisible by $3$ are possible?',r'''The possibilities are $0,18,19,55$. Two unit triangles sharing an edge have congruent opposite entries modulo $3$. Propagating this relation shows that all entries belong to three repeating residue classes. In coordinates $(r,c)$ with $0\le c\le r\le9$, these are the classes of $r+c$ modulo $3$, containing $19,18,18$ points.

If their assigned residues are $u,v,w$, the triangle condition is exactly $u+v+w\equiv0\pmod3$. Either all three residues are zero, none is zero, or precisely one is zero. (Two zero residues force the third to vanish.) These cases give exactly the four listed counts, each attained by choosing suitable $u,v,w$.''','Local modular constraints must be propagated through the entire lattice and its three colour-class sizes counted.')
add(2019,6,4.5,'Geometry',['coordinates','tangents','circles'],r'Let $ABCD$ be a square and $P$ a point of its circumcircle on the minor arc $CD$, excluding the endpoints. Set $X=AP\cap BD$, $Y=CP\cap BD$, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $XY$. Prove that $MP$ is tangent to the circle.',r'''Scale and rotate so that $A=(0,-1),B=(-1,0),C=(0,1),D=(1,0)$. Write $P=(u,v)$, where $u^2+v^2=1$ and $u,v>0$. Intersecting $AP$ and $CP$ with the horizontal axis gives
$$X=\left(\frac u{1+v},0\right),\qquad Y=\left(\frac u{1-v},0\right).$$
Their midpoint is $M=(1/u,0)$. Hence
$$P\cdot(M-P)=u(1/u-u)-v^2=1-u^2-v^2=0.$$
Thus $MP$ is perpendicular to the radius through $P$, as required.''','The midpoint-of-hypotenuse geometry or a short circle-coordinate calculation reveals the tangent relation.')
add(2019,7,4.5,'Algebra',['sequences','discrete concavity','integer bounds'],r'Integers $a_1,\ldots,a_{100}$ satisfy $a_1=a_{100}=0$ and $2a_i>a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}$ for $2\le i\le99$. Find the least possible value of $a_{19}$.',r'''Put $d_i=a_{i+1}-a_i$. These are strictly decreasing integers with sum zero. Set $e_i=d_i-(50-i)$ for $1\le i\le99$. Since $d_i-d_{i+1}\ge1$, the sequence $e_i$ is nonincreasing; its sum is zero because $49+48+\cdots-49=0$.

The mean of the first eighteen terms of a nonincreasing sequence is at least its overall mean. Therefore $\sum_{i=1}^{18}e_i\ge0$, and
$$a_{19}=\sum_{i=1}^{18}d_i\ge49+48+\cdots+32=729.$$
Equality is attained by taking $d_i=50-i$ and defining $a_i$ by cumulative sums.''','Strict integer concavity becomes a monotone slope sequence; subtracting the minimal unit slope gap gives the sharp bound.')
